WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.990 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the box said  leave it in for 10 to 15 minutes i was  in my early 20s  slathering relaxer on my hair in the  bathroom of my small dc apartment  willing it to be as straight as possible  and thinking why not leave it in for 20.  my beauty philosophy was like my cooking  philosophy a little less here  a little extra there except as it turns  out altering the texture of your hair  with a chemical relaxer is  not cooking it's signs and in science if  you don't follow a procedure to a t  it turns out things can and will go  wrong  it was just a few days later that i was  standing in front of that same bathroom  mirror looking at a head of  rough limp over processed hair  each time i ran my comb through it  several strands would break  and fall to the cold tile floor now i  know what you're thinking  i know where the story goes she cuts it  all off starts anew  and now she's rocking that dope set of  curls i had to cut it right  i didn't for weeks i continued to wash  and straighten that head of damaged hair  each morning as i got ready for work i  would watch as more and more strands  fell to the ground as i combed and i did  nothing  i did nothing because i didn't know what  else there was to do  even in the face of irreversible damage  i could not picture a me  without straight hair i was already  curvier than most  my complexion dark the last foothold i  had in the realm of what i consider to  be beautiful was that head of hair and  without it  who was i going to be  here i was in my early 20s having  something of an identity crisis  for most of my life i had adhered to  certain standards of beauty that had  been ingrained into me by the media  my experiences and those around me that  if my hair was  longer straighter my skin lighter my  body a different shape  that i would be more worthy more loved  that i would be beautiful  even if you've never clung desperately  to straight hair the way i did  there are certain beauty standards that  many of us will never meet  yet feel compelled to aspire to what i  want you to remember is this  better beauty is diverse and inclusive  beauty  today i'm going to explain how beauty is  failing all of us  from our beauty standards to our retail  ecosystem  using my own tumultuous hair journey  so eventually my hair hit the point  where i had to do something  it's how i ended up in the salon just a  few weeks later listening to my stylist  suggest that i go forth with a big chop  and wear my natural hair twas or teeny  weeny afros were in  but in afro i couldn't do it  that couldn't have been any farther from  what i'd learned to be beautiful as a  child  you see in our society eurocentric  features are the gold standard in beauty  we value thinner longer hair lighter  skin  and a certain body type over all others  while beauty standards cause all of us  harm they disproportionately work  against black and brown women  my hair as it grows out of my head those  kinky coily curls my stylist love so  much or my richly hued skin  have not only been implicitly rejected  but explicitly devalued  during slavery the cutting of the hair  of slaves was routine  and slave masters continually expressed  disgust at the sight of black hair  in the 19th century black hair was often  not even called hair  rather it was labeled and ridiculed as  wool  this was the beginning of a binary  system of good hair  and bad hair good hair  was manageable and neat it was white  hair  bad hair was nappy and uncontrollable  it was black hair  that ideal has permeated our society  and influences what we consider to be  appropriate and acceptable across  every sphere of our lives that's no  better proven  than in a duke university study  published just last year  where two groups evaluated the same  black female candidate  the first group saw that candidate with  straight hair and the second group  with curly hair that first group rated  that candidate  as more professional and more strongly  recommended for an interview  can you imagine losing out on a  promotion because you chose to embrace  your authentic self  it's not just a loss for black women  like me it's a loss for  the organizations that we're a part of  our beauty standards  they're unhealthy restrictive even  racist  they don't serve you or me for that  matter  remember this better beauty is diverse  and inclusive beauty  so eventually i made the decision to  slowly let my natural hair grow out and  cut off the relaxed hair  the hardest part of my journey i thought  was over  except i had just encountered the real  struggle  shopping for beauty products i would  walk into the local sephora eager to  find something that would cater to my  newly emerging curls  as i moved past walls of homogeneous  advertisements dotted with one or two  models of color  i would feel that hot stink of  non-belonging creep up into my chest  that was only reinforced as i shopped  i'd scan products aisle by aisle trying  not to succumb to the frustration that  comes with limited options  all while being hyper aware of the  associates who would follow me around  close enough to make sure i didn't steal  but far enough to make sure they didn't  have to answer my questions  you can look at retail and see that  beauty is failing us  in a study commission by sephora just  last year  over 74 percent of shoppers feel that  beauty marketing fails to show a diverse  range of skin tones  hair textures and body types and over  half of black shoppers have experienced  bias or unfair treatment on the basis of  race or skin color  this is a fact that is contradictory to  the impact that black and brown women  have on the beauty market as a whole  black people for instance make up just  15 of the population  yet we make up nearly 22 of the us  beauty market  and black and brown women on average buy  beauty products more frequently  and in greater quantities than any other  demographic  you'd expect with such an outsized  contribution  that beauty retailers would be clamoring  to carry the brands that serve us  many of which are created by black and  brown founders and yet today  only three percent three percent  of sephora's brands are black owned  so why does beauty retail matter so much  the beauty industry gains its power  by telling our society what we deem to  be attractive and valuable  and what is not psychological  manipulation is the foundation upon  which beauty is built  what you see when you go into target to  replace that shampoo  or run into sephora to get a new  foundation it matters  and if you're like me and you struggle  to find the products that meet your  needs  or aren't represented in the ads you see  you begin to believe that you're not  just  invisible but that you're physically  undesirable  in my little studio apartment i had felt  lost as to who i was  but let me tell you about the day i met  myself  i was 25 back in that same seat in my  silas salon  shrouded behind a thick mask of  self-doubt she had been impossible to  find  but that day as my stylist placed that  last curl and a head of now fully  natural hair  i caught a glimpse of a woman i hadn't  known before  she was bold she was proud  but most importantly she attacked an  endless well of self-love and  self-confidence that came from  pushing aside a broken beauty system and  finally  finally feeling comfortable in her own  skin  now let me be clear this isn't a story  about finding freedom by moving from  relaxed to natural hair  this is a story about making room for  all of who we are  by stepping outside of a box that none  of us should have to live by  and that that is the story we all  deserve to have  it's the reason why i'm building  beautocracy  a platform that elevates diverse and  inclusive beauty and makes it easier for  women of color to shop and discover skin  care  in an environment that reflects their  worth  our community is built on the idea that  better beauty is about giving back the  power of choice  in the retail environment of creating  space for those around us to achieve  more without being held back by bias and  expanding the narrative so that we can  all  live our own complex stories  what i want you to remember is this  better beauty is diverse and inclusive  beauty  there are a treasure trove of beauty  brands online created by black and brown  founders with products that work for all  of us  go online search black on beauty brands  discover something new  and incorporate it into your routine  diversify  the brands and the influencers that you  follow on social media  and demand that retailers like sephora  and ulta who have committed to  diversifying their shelves and making  the retail environment more inclusive  actually follow through  my difficult hair journey to self-love  and acceptance  it isn't unique but if we work together  it could be as consumers  we have the power to step outside of the  box that we've been given  and change beauty for the better

